    "> *Your answer here:*\n",
    "1. If Pearson filter and RFE did not choose exactly the same top 4 features, it shows that Pearson only checks simple linear correlation with the target one feature at a time, while RFE checks how useful features are together inside a model. So Pearson can miss features that are weak alone but useful when combined with others.\n",
    "2. You must fit StandardScaler only on the training set because using the full dataset would leak information from the test set into training and make the model evaluation too optimistic.\n",
    "3. Lasso selects features by shrinking some coefficients exactly to zero during model training, while RFE repeatedly trains a model and removes the least important features step by step.\n",
    "4. If the single train/test split R² and 5-Fold CV mean R² are close, the model performance is fairly stable. If they are very different, the single split may be unreliable. The CV standard deviation shows how much the R² changes across folds: a small std means stable performance, while a large std means the result depends a lot on which data ended up in the train/test split."
